'''The Kurupiran languages''', alternatively known as '''Kurupirangüe''' ({{Literal translation|forest language}} {{W|IPA|/kuru'piraŋɡwe/}}), form an [[Abyala|Abyalan]] [[W:Language family|language family]], spoken by the [[Kurupiranga|Kurupiranga peoples]] of the [[Baliscan archipelago]]. The languages of the Kurupiranga have been determined to have descended from the [[W:Je–Tupi–Carib languages|Korobiri-Kurupiran languages]], and are considered to be close relatives of the now extinct [[Maipùri language]], which was lost in the 16th century as a result of the [[Colonial History of Burawa|Burawi colonization of Abyala]].

The Kurupiran language family is one of the best-documented and most studied in [[Algarves|the Algarves]]. As a result of the dispersal of Indigenous populations across the [[Marañón basin]], the Kurupiran languages would diverge into five distinct branches, including [[Tapajóara language|Tapajóara]], [[Kuarupíba language|Kuarupíba]], [[Aruanãyára languages|Aruanãyára]], [[Guarani languages|Guarani]] and [[Kalútupí languages|Kalútupí]].

The Kurupiran languages are some of the most widely spoken {{W|Indigenous languages of the Americas|Abyalan languages}}, and remain commonly used among the [[Kurupiranga people]] and neighboring communities. This is unique among Abyalan languages; [[W:Language shift|language shift]] towards [[Illypnia|Illypnian]] or [[Fosia|Fosian]] colonial languages (in this case, Xacano, Volisanian and Ajami) has otherwise been a nearly universal phenomenon in the Western Hemisphere, but Indigenous communities in the  have maintained their traditional language while also adopting the languages of the Mesogean colonizers.

